From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 11:17:50 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] kernelbase: Merge the user's classes into HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT,
 as Windows does.

HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT only showed HKLM\Software\Classes, so a class, ProgID
or file association registered for the user alone was not found through
it.  What Windows does is in advapi32's test_classesroot tests, which
stopped at their first todo_wine: a handle opened through HKCR is marked
((handle & 3) == 2); its values and subkeys are looked up on the user's
side first, again at every call, and on the machine's otherwise; a key
that is on neither side is created in HKLM; RegDeleteKey removes the
user's key first; enumeration lists the user's entries and then those
only the machine has; and enumerating a handle whose key was deleted
fails with ERROR_KEY_DELETED.

A marked handle is the real one with bit 1 set, which the server
ignores; every entry point strips it before a Nt* call.  Whether each
marked handle is the user's key or the machine's, and its path relative
to Classes, is kept until RegCloseKey, so a key's name is asked for at
most once; the machine's key is used through the handle itself, and
only the user's side is looked up again at each call, one server call.
A Wow6432Node walk on the user's side first looks for the path's first
component, as most users' Classes are nearly empty: Office's
Click-to-Run integrator opens some twenty thousand keys through HKCR
with KEY_WOW64_32KEY at every start, and walking both sides in full
took it from 18 to 28 seconds.  Enumeration keeps, per handle, how far
it got in the machine's entries, so going through HKCR's thousands of
keys stays linear, and RegQueryInfoKey counts the entries both sides
have by going through the smaller side.  An override of
HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT is a plain key, as it was.  That a query falls back
to the machine's value, that RegQueryInfoKey counts the merged set, and
what an override does on Windows are not measured yet.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

